    thinking: "**Refining Focus: Active Element**\n\nI\'ve hit a snag. My initial assumption about `document.activeElement` might be flawed for cascading dropdowns. What if the active element isn\'t what triggered the change? I\'m considering using `onchange` explicitly now.\n\n\n**Revising Strategy: Event Handling**\n\nI\'m now thinking I should explicitly handle the `onWilayaChange()` event, rather than attempting to intercept changes within `applyFilters`. This seems more straightforward. Additionally, I need to account for the fact that `wilayaEl.value` changes when a state is selected. I was mistakenly thinking that `applyFilters` automatically recalculated `allowedPlaces`, which isn\'t true unless `populateWilayaFilter` is called. So, the new strategy is to call `populateWilayaFilter()` every time `applyFilters()` is run, but now I\'m worried about the flickering that the innerHTML replacement creates. Finally, a better thought occurred, to separate the population of Wilaya and Commune options, and update Commune options only when Wilaya changes.\n\n\n**Validating Assumptions: Trigger**\n\nOkay, so I\'ve validated that using `document.activeElement` for the triggering element is, in fact, correct, even with the `onchange` event. I was concerned about the programmatic setting of `selC.value` potentially triggering `onchange`, but that isn\'t the case, so I don\'t need to worry there. I\'m now certain that setting a select to `\'\'` doesn\'t trigger a change event, which simplifies things.\n\n\n**Analyzing Data: Wilaya Processing**\n\nI\'ve been re-examining how I\'m handling the \"Wilaya\" data. I\'ve realized a potential logic flaw in how I\'m processing the state and place components. Specifically, I\'m concerned about the conditional assignment of `state` and `place` when splitting the \"Wilaya\" string. I had forgotten to `.trim()` a fallback case, which could lead to inconsistencies. Now, I\'m tracing the exact values to ensure \"Alger\" is handled consistently as a `state` value. I\'m also remembering to consider the filtering problems.\n\n\n**Evaluating Logic: Filter Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical filter conflict. My immediate concern is that I\'m not matching HTML value of options to the expected data. I need to make sure I\'m using the exact string values for comparisons like `cat && r.category !== cat`. I\'ve remembered that I have to handle undefined values, so I\'m accounting for that, especially with the `archived` property.\n\n\n**Clarifying Data: Status Values**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring data consistency for the \'status\' values. I\'ve realized a discrepancy: the values used in the HTML options, `src/app.js`, and the database entries (mock data) aren\'t aligned. Specifically, the database uses \'fixed\', while the UI options use \'\342\234\205 \331\205\331\217\330\265\331\204\330\255\'. I need to map these values correctly for filtering, ensuring a match for the \'status\' property, as well as accounting for the admin interface updates. I have to think of updating the filter matching on the fly.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
