  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Municipalities Now**\n\nI\'m now diving into the filter functionality, specifically assessing the breadth of municipalities displayed. My attention has turned to how the application handles Nominatim\'s responses in `app.js` during report saving, ensuring correct formatting for location strings when a municipality is identified and distinct from the state.\n\n\n**Refining Wilaya Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the wilaya extraction. It appears the current implementation in `app.js` correctly formats location strings like \"Bir Mourad Rais, Alger\". The issue arises when splitting this into `parts`, I realize. We\'re extracting a `place` (empty) and `state` as \"Alger\" because mock data has only single-part entries. Currently, I\'m working to fine-tune how we filter by Commune (`rPlace`), building upon my last insights.\n\n\n**Validating Filter Logic**\n\nI\'ve just validated the filter logic, particularly concerning edge cases with states having no communes, like \"Oran.\" The system handles these scenarios flawlessly. I\'m now exploring the user feedback about missing municipalities in the admin dashboard. It seems there was an issue with the commune filter\'s population when the wilaya filter was cleared, something I need to fully investigate.\n\n\n**Addressing Filter Persistence**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a crucial edge case. The `f-commune` filter wasn\'t being correctly updated after a filter reset, particularly when the active element isn\'t the wilaya element. This meant the commune dropdown wouldn\'t refresh to show all municipalities, or the correct corresponding ones. I\'m now implementing a fix that unconditionally calls `updateCommunesFilter()` in `resetFilters()` to ensure the filter resets properly.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 504988913
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
