  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Translation and Errors**\n\nI\'ve realized the \"Reports\" page, which is \"\330\247\331\204\330\264\331\203\330\247\331\210\331\211\", has broken filters for wilaya and commune. It seems I might have introduced this bug in the previous iteration. The user is still unable to filter by municipality on both \"\331\204\331\210\330\255\330\251 \330\247\331\204\331\202\331\212\330\247\330\257\330\251\" and \"\330\247\331\204\330\264\331\203\330\247\331\210\331\211.\" Also, the user is wondering who can confirm or signal the admins.\n\n\n**Investigating Filter Malfunction**\n\nI\'m now digging into the \"\330\247\331\204\330\264\331\203\330\247\331\210\331\211\" page\'s commune filter. I was looking at the code, specifically `src/admin.js:520`, and I see the filter is implemented, and the `applyFilters` is indeed filtering by `f-commune`. However, the communes aren\'t populating. I\'m now examining how `allReports.forEach` populates `knownPlaces` and `knownStates` to understand how the municipality information gets lost, and why communes are seemingly absent from the list. It looks like the presence or absence of a comma in `r.wilaya` plays a role in what gets used.\n\n\n**Clarifying Municipality Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how municipality data is handled in the system, specifically concerning the admin dashboard filters. I\'m investigating how `r.wilaya` gets saved and processed, especially after reviewing the Nominatim API calls in `src/app.js:1018`. It appears older reports or reports created with mock data may lack the detailed municipality information needed for effective filtering. The user wants the dropdown to show all municipalities, which likely necessitates a predefined list or other data source to ensure comprehensiveness.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 913585975
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
